    <item>
      <title>Hash Literals in Ruby 3.1</title>
      <link>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/hash-literals-in-ruby-3-1/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 06 Aug 2026 09:21:34 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/hash-literals-in-ruby-3-1/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;Starting with Ruby 3.1, you can &lt;a href=&#34;https://rubyreferences.github.io/rubychanges/3.1.html#values-in-hash-literals-and-keyword-arguments-can-be-omitted&#34;  class=&#34;external-link&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;omit values in hash literals&lt;/a&gt;, meaning you can write:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-fallback&#34; data-lang=&#34;fallback&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;a = &amp;#39;some value&amp;#39;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;b = &amp;#39;other value&amp;#39;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;h = { a:, b: }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;In my opinion, this is one of the coolest syntax features, and I use it all the time when building apps.&#xA;However, I almost always catch myself asking: should you always name block variables to match the hash key?&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-fallback&#34; data-lang=&#34;fallback&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;# 1.&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;1.upto(10).each do |value|&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;  hash = { value: }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;  # other code&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;end&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;# 2.&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;1.upto(10).each do |i|&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;  hash = { value: i }&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;  # other code&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;end&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Have you ever thought about this trade-off? Which style do you prefer?&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Fallback value for hash in Ruby</title>
      <link>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/fallback-value-for-hash-in-ruby/</link>
      <pubDate>Thu, 30 Jul 2026 08:33:54 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/fallback-value-for-hash-in-ruby/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;When building applications in Ruby, almost every developer works with hashes on a daily basis.&#xA;We especially run into nested hashes all the time, for example:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-ruby&#34; data-lang=&#34;ruby&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;u&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;user&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;id&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;lang&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;id&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;name&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;Ruby&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;u&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;user&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;lang&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;name&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;# =&amp;gt; &amp;#34;Ruby&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;Chaining keys like this looks cool, but it only works as long as every key actually holds a value:&lt;/p&gt;&#xA;&lt;div class=&#34;highlight&#34;&gt;&lt;pre tabindex=&#34;0&#34; class=&#34;chroma&#34;&gt;&lt;code class=&#34;language-ruby&#34; data-lang=&#34;ruby&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;u&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;user&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;id&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;mi&#34;&gt;2&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;lang&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;kp&#34;&gt;nil&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;p&#34;&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;line&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;cl&#34;&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;n&#34;&gt;u&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;user&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;lang&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;][&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;s1&#34;&gt;&amp;#39;name&amp;#39;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class=&#34;o&#34;&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class=&#34;c1&#34;&gt;# undefined method &amp;#39;[]&amp;#39; for nil (NoMethodError)&lt;/span&gt;&#xA;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;To fix this error, you could use the &lt;a href=&#34;https://docs.ruby-lang.org/en/master/Object.html#method-i-send&#34;  class=&#34;external-link&#34; target=&#34;_blank&#34; rel=&#34;noopener&#34;&gt;&lt;code&gt;send&lt;/code&gt; method&lt;/a&gt; (though it looks pretty terrifying):&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
    <item>
      <title>Pass keyword arguments to a method in Ruby</title>
      <link>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/pass-keyword-arguments-to-a-method-in-ruby/</link>
      <pubDate>Tue, 05 May 2026 07:38:19 +0000</pubDate>
      <guid>https://yegorov.github.io/notes/pass-keyword-arguments-to-a-method-in-ruby/</guid>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;If you work with Ruby, you&amp;rsquo;ve often encountered a situation where a method takes multiple keyword arguments. And you need to pass a hash to it and also add another new argument.&#xA;In most cases, you do the following: create a new hash and pass it as an argument to the method using the double splat operator &lt;code&gt;**&lt;/code&gt;. But you can do this more concisely: instead of creating a new hash, pass the hash followed by another argument.&#xA;Furthermore, if you need a different order, you can pass the arguments in reverse order. If the argument keys are the same, the last argument will overwrite the first.&lt;/p&gt;</description>
    </item>
